rules_version = '2';

// Showrunner — Firestore security rules (spec 09 §3, spec 04 §2, spec 02 §1).
//
// These rules are the *enforcement* half of "judgment by agents, enforcement by policy" (spec 04 §1).
// Agents write opinions: an aesthetic score, a dignity verdict, a face match. One deterministic,
// transactional function — `recompute_visibility`, the only writer of `media.visibility` anywhere in
// the system — turns those opinions plus the uploader's consent ring into a single field. Everything
// below serves strictly what that field already decided. No language model can widen exposure by
// being wrong, because no rule here consults a model's output directly.
//
// Five properties are worth checking against the code before changing anything here:
//
//  1. **Identity is custom claims only** (`personId`, `hosts`, `members`, `platformAdmin`) — set
//     server-side by `api` after enrollment / magic-link redemption / `POST /join` / a CLI grant,
//     never self-asserted. No rule performs a `get()`: rules allow only ten per request and each is
//     billed, so a `get()`-based album or membership check would cap a gallery grid at ten photos.
//     `hosts` and `members` are **arrays** — a host runs more than one event over a season and a
//     guest attends more than one event on one phone, and a scalar claim silently revoked the first
//     of each (`backend/shared/auth.py`'s module docstring). Firebase caps custom claims at 1000
//     bytes for the whole object, which puts a ceiling of roughly 32 events per array; the server
//     refuses the 33rd rather than truncating, because truncating would revoke an event at random.
//     The old scalar `host` claim is still honoured by `isHost()` so tokens already minted in the
//     wild keep working until they expire.
//  2. **Clients never write anything except reactions.** Uploads register through the API (which
//     shape-validates and rate-limits), consent flips through the API, and every perception write is
//     a service account outside these rules entirely. Spec 04 §2 permits client-written upload-intent
//     docs; this implementation is tighter, and tighter is fine.
//  3. **A public read needs `visibility=='public'` AND `status=='indexed'`.** Both, always — spec 04
//     §2. Visibility can flip to `public` a second or two before the last stage lands, so the status
//     term is what stops a half-processed photo from appearing on a wall. Every public-surface query
//     in `frontend/src/lib/firestore.ts` carries both filters for exactly this reason: a Firestore
//     query whose constraints do not guarantee the read rule fails *entirely*, so query filters and
//     rules are one design, not two.
//  4. **Biometrics are unreachable from any client.** `faces/` (512-d face embeddings) and
//     `enrollments/` (selfie templates) have no allow rule at all. Person documents deliberately do
//     *not* hold the embedding, because a rule cannot grant one field of a document and withhold
//     another, and the kiosk credit chip, the leaderboard's names and the Highlights tier ranking all
//     need to read person documents. See `backend/shared/fs.py::enrollments_col`.
//  5. **Membership is event-scoped, and it is a boundary, not a formality.** `isMember(eventId)` used
//     to be `signedIn()` with no argument, which meant an anonymous session holding *any* eventId
//     could read that event's public media, its `people` names and tiers, its `guests` leaderboard,
//     its `bounties` and its published `reels`. Every event was a public event protected only by a
//     128-bit link. Membership is now a claim minted by `POST /v1/events/{eventId}/join`, which on an
//     invite-only event demands the host's hashed code and takes a seat under a transactional cap.
//     Nothing about consent changed: `recompute_visibility` still has exactly its old inputs and is
//     still the only writer of `media.visibility`. Membership decides *who is at the event*; the
//     rings decide *what they may see*. The two compose and neither is expressed in the other.
//
// **Residual exposure, stated rather than papered over.** Two things stay readable more widely than
// membership, and both are deliberate:
//
//  - `events/{id}/kiosk/{document}` is `allow read: if true`, spec 09 §3 verbatim, and it cannot be
//    tightened here: deciding by access mode would need a `get()` on the event document. The playlist
//    holds mediaIds, stage ids, a theme string and the publisher's own score factors — no names, no
//    uids, no captions, no bytes. Reading it tells an outsider that photo `01J…` is currently on some
//    wall and nothing else, because every collection it points *into* is member-gated by the rules
//    below, and `api/media.py` refuses the unauthenticated byte path on an invite-only event. A
//    private event's kiosk therefore renders an empty programme to a non-member.
//  - A member reads whole `media` documents, which carry `albumOf` (which personIds are in the frame),
//    `subjectVetoes`, the Guardian verdict and the per-item `usage` cost. Splitting the media document
//    to hide those was considered and deliberately not done: it is a restructuring of the one document
//    every worker in the fleet writes to, two days from a deadline, and the fields are event-internal
//    rather than personal — `albumOf` holds opaque personIds, and the display names they map to are
//    already on the wall. Written down here so it is a known shape and not a surprise.
//
// Anything without an explicit `allow` is denied: that is the default, and it covers `hashes/`
// (dedupe register), `claimLinks/` and `hostLinks/` (magic-link and invite-code hashes),
// `publisherLease/` and `ticks/` (the two control-plane leases), `platform/` (caps, kill switch, tick
// heartbeat) and every collection a future session adds before it thinks about rules.
//
// Verified by `rules-tests/run_matrix.py` (`make rules-test`) against the Firestore emulator.

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- identity (claims only)

    function signedIn() {
      return request.auth != null;
    }

    function uid() {
      return signedIn() ? request.auth.uid : '';
    }

    // Membership in **this** event, from the `members` array claim that
    // `POST /v1/events/{eventId}/join` mints on the caller's own uid (`backend/api/membership.py`).
    //
    // This used to be `signedIn()` with no argument, on the reasoning that anonymous sign-in *is* the
    // act of arriving at an event (spec 02 §1) and that nothing granted on that basis was sensitive.
    // The first half was true and the second half was the mistake: an eventId is not a secret — it is
    // in a QR code, a URL and a kiosk address bar — so "signed in" let anyone who had ever seen one
    // read that event's public wall, its guest list's points and every display name and VIP tier at
    // it. An array claim rather than a membership document because no rule here may `get()` (see
    // property 1); an array rather than a scalar because one phone attends more than one event.
    //
    // Hosting implies membership, and the platform operator is a member of every event (spec 11 §1.1
    // is explicit that the operator exists to support events they do not host). Neither implies the
    // other, and both are checked here rather than repeated at ~5 call sites below.
    function isMember(eventId) {
      return isHost(eventId)
        || isAdmin()
        || (signedIn() && eventId in request.auth.token.get('members', []));
    }

    // `hosts` is the array; the bare `host` string is the claim shape this system minted before a
    // second event proved a scalar wrong, honoured so a console open mid-event survives the change
    // (an ID token lasts an hour). Every new grant writes the array and clears the scalar.
    function isHost(eventId) {
      return signedIn()
        && (eventId in request.auth.token.get('hosts', [])
            || request.auth.token.get('host', '') == eventId);
    }

    // Minted once via CLI for the deployment owner (spec 11 §1.1). Never granted to a guest, and
    // never a substitute for the host claim on someone else's event — it exists so the operator can
    // support and seed events they are not the host of.
    function isAdmin() {
      return signedIn() && request.auth.token.platformAdmin == true;
    }

    function claimedPerson() {
      return signedIn() && 'personId' in request.auth.token ? request.auth.token.personId : '';
    }

    function isPerson(personId) {
      return claimedPerson() != '' && claimedPerson() == personId;
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- events

    match /events/{eventId} {
      // Host-only: the event document carries `demoConfig`, `class`, `publicFloor`, `costSoFarUsd`
      // and the platform caps. Guest apps get the projection they actually need — name, theme, stage
      // chips, glossary — from `GET /v1/events/{eventId}/public`, which is a deliberate boundary and
      // not an oversight.
      allow read: if isHost(eventId) || isAdmin();
      allow write: if false;

      // ---------------------------------------------------------------- media (the whole point)

      match /media/{mediaId} {
        function media() {
          return resource.data;
        }

        // Ring 2 + Guardian + quality floor + no subject veto, all already collapsed into
        // `visibility` by `recompute_visibility`. `status=='indexed'` means every stage landed.
        // `isMember(eventId)` is spec 04 §2's "any *authed* event member may read" — the one place
        // spec 09 §3's shorter skeleton and spec 04 §2 differ, and the more specific one wins.
        // Anonymous sign-in happens before anything renders (spec 02 §1), including on the kiosk, and
        // `POST /join` runs on the same page load, so this costs an open event's surfaces nothing; it
        // does mean a bare `curl` of a photo document gets nothing, and that an eventId scraped from
        // someone else's QR code is now worth nothing on its own.
        function isPubliclyVisible() {
          return isMember(eventId)
            && media().get('visibility', '') == 'public'
            && media().get('status', '') == 'indexed';
        }

        // A person in the frame reads their own photos (spec 04 §2's pool tier). `albumOf` is the
        // membership array the Face Indexer maintains, because Firestore cannot OR-query. The
        // visibility test excludes `self`: a Ring-0 upload stays with its uploader even though the
        // subject is still listed in `albumOf` — and it excludes `blocked` items for the same reason,
        // since those are forced to `self` at the gate.
        function isSubject() {
          return claimedPerson() != ''
            && claimedPerson() in media().get('albumOf', [])
            && media().get('visibility', '') in ['pool', 'public'];
        }

        allow read: if isHost(eventId)
          || isAdmin()
          || (signedIn() && media().get('uploaderUid', '') == uid())
          || isPubliclyVisible()
          || isSubject();

        // Every mutation — consent flips, subject vetoes, host decisions, perception results —
        // goes through the API or a worker service account. Both write outside these rules.
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // ---------------------------------------------------------------- people

      match /people/{personId} {
        // Display name, `tier`, `featured`, enrollment state — by construction nothing else. The
        // selfie template lives in `enrollments/{personId}` (no rule, below) and everything else
        // private about a person lives in `private/profile` (deny-all, immediately below). What a
        // member can read here is what the kiosk already puts on a five-metre screen: the
        // leaderboard's names and the deterministic tier→vipWeight multiplier behind Highlights
        // ordering (spec 04 §4, spec 11 §3.3).
        allow read: if isMember(eventId);
        allow write: if false;

        // Deny-all, and no longer merely reserved. Firestore cannot grant one field of a document and
        // withhold another, and the parent document above has to stay member-readable, so anything
        // genuinely private about a person has to physically live down here:
        //   - `uidLinks` — the uid↔person join table (spec 02 §1). Readable, it maps every anonymous
        //     session at the event to a named human, which is the exact correlation anonymous
        //     sign-in exists to prevent.
        //   - `tasteProfile` / `tasteMemo` / `tasteMemoAt` / `lastMemoReactionCount` — spec 07 §2's
        //     affinity vector and the Gemma-written memo about what this guest likes. Prose about a
        //     person's taste, on a document every member of their event could read, was the most
        //     personal text exposed anywhere in this system.
        // Written and read only with service credentials (`backend/shared/fs.py::person_private_ref`).
        // Deny-all includes the host: a console has no use for either, and "the host can see it" is
        // how something like this ends up in a screenshot.
        match /private/{document=**} {
          allow read, write: if false;
        }

        // "A new device joined your album — not you? tap here" (spec 02 §3.2). Addressed to exactly
        // one person, so exactly one person reads it.
        match /notices/{noticeId} {
          allow read: if isPerson(personId) || isHost(eventId);
          allow write: if false;
        }

        // The one client write in the entire system (spec 09 §3, verbatim). Deletes are allowed for
        // the owner too — un-loving a photo is the same right as loving it — but a delete carries no
        // `request.resource`, so it needs its own rule rather than riding the shape check.
        match /reactions/{reactionId} {
          allow read: if isPerson(personId) || isHost(eventId);
          allow create, update: if isPerson(personId)
            && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['verdict', 'at'])
            && request.resource.data.verdict in ['love', 'hide'];
          allow delete: if isPerson(personId);
        }
      }

      // ---------------------------------------------------------------- biometrics: no client, ever

      // 512-d face embeddings, one document per detected face. Unreadable by every client including
      // the host: an embedding is not something a console needs, and "the host can see it" is how a
      // biometric ends up in a screenshot. `worker-face` and `api` read it with service credentials.
      match /faces/{faceId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // Selfie templates (spec 02 §4's biometric consent artefact). Same reasoning, stronger case.
      match /enrollments/{personId} {
        allow read, write: if false;
      }

      // ---------------------------------------------------------------- the live surfaces

      match /guests/{guestUid} {
        // Points and upload counts — the leaderboard slot on the kiosk (spec 04 §4) reads this
        // collection directly, ordered by points. No email, no phone, no token: a uid is not a
        // credential, and the identity that matters is the one in the verified ID token. Member-scoped
        // because the guest list *is* the headcount: readable across events, it would let anyone
        // holding an eventId enumerate how many sessions an event has and who is winning it.
        allow read: if isMember(eventId);
        allow write: if false;

        // Deny-all, and it is what makes the "no token" sentence above literally true rather than
        // aspirational. Web Push arrived with something that genuinely had to be stored per session —
        // an FCM registration token — and a token is an *address*: anything holding it is holding a
        // way to reach that specific device. This collection is streamed in full by the kiosk
        // leaderboard, so a token kept on the parent document would have been handed to every other
        // guest at the event alongside the display name it sat next to.
        //
        // Same shape and same reasoning as `people/{personId}/private/{document=**}` above; written
        // by `api/push.py` and read by `directors/story/act.py`'s bounty writer, both with service
        // credentials (`backend/shared/fs.py::guest_push_ref`). Deny-all includes the host, who has
        // no use for a guest's device address and every chance of putting one in a screenshot.
        match /private/{document=**} {
          allow read, write: if false;
        }
      }

      match /bounties/{bountyId} {
        // Spec 09 §3: readable by event members. A bounty is a public mission — it goes on the wall
        // as a wanted poster and into every phone's banner, so there is nothing to withhold from a
        // member. From a non-member there is: a brief names a stage, a moment and sometimes a person.
        allow read: if isMember(eventId);
        allow write: if false;
      }

      match /reels/{reelId} {
        // Published reels are media-like (spec 04 §5): public only once every constituent asset is
        // still Ring-2-eligible, which `recompute_visibility` has already collapsed into the same
        // `visibility` field. Unpublished drafts and superseded versions stay with the host.
        //
        // The `visibility == 'public'` branch carried **no** signed-in term at all until the event
        // boundary landed — the one member-readable collection in the tree that a wholly
        // unauthenticated caller could read, which made it the cheapest way to confirm an event
        // existed and enumerate its finished films. `isMember(eventId)` fixes that and matches the
        // `media` rule above term for term; `api/reels.py` applies the same test to the video bytes.
        allow read: if isHost(eventId)
          || isAdmin()
          || (isMember(eventId) && resource.data.get('visibility', '') == 'public');
        allow write: if false;
      }

      match /kiosk/{document} {
        // Spec 09 §3, verbatim: `allow read: if true`. The kiosk is a TV in a venue and the playlist
        // holds only mediaIds that are already public plus the publisher's own ranking factors; making
        // the show depend on an auth session would be a way for the wall to go dark, not a control.
        //
        // It stays `true` even for an invite-only event, and this is the honest reason: tightening it
        // would require reading `events/{eventId}.access.mode`, and that is a `get()` — the one thing
        // property 1 forbids. What actually makes a private event's wall dark is that every collection
        // this document points into (`media`, `people`, `guests`, `bounties`, `reels`) is member-gated
        // above, and that `api/media.py` and `api/reels.py` refuse the unauthenticated byte path when
        // the mode is `invite`. A non-member reading this document gets a programme of opaque ULIDs it
        // cannot resolve into a name, a caption or a pixel. The venue TV becomes a member through a
        // kiosk link (`POST /v1/events/{eventId}/kiosk-links`), which grants `members` and never
        // `hosts`. `access.kioskPublic` is honoured by the kiosk client, not by this rule — it is a
        // host's "don't put this event on a wall at all" switch, not a security boundary, and it is
        // labelled as such in the console.
        allow read: if true;
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // ---------------------------------------------------------------- host-only operations

      // Quarantines, DLQ deliveries, Model Armor deflections, and moderation decisions — all
      // host-authed, so one rule covers the whole subtree.
      match /ops/{document=**} {
        allow read: if isHost(eventId) || isAdmin();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Who claimed which faces, how, when (spec 02 §3.2) — the audit trail that makes a wrong claim
      // visible and reversible. Host-visible by design, guest-invisible for the same reason.
      match /claimAudits/{claimId} {
        allow read: if isHost(eventId) || isAdmin();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // The Story Director's aggregate state (spec 05 §1): `ledger/directorState` (the rolling 10-tick
      // window, the permanent coverage gaps, the deferred reel commissions) and
      // `ledger/coverageShards/stages/{stageId}` (the incremental counters). Recursive because the
      // shards are a subcollection — a single-segment match would grant the parent document and deny
      // the counters under it, which is how a host console ends up showing an empty coverage grid
      // against a working ledger. Host-only either way: this is the director's reasoning surfaced, and
      // a guest has no use for how thin the coverage of a stage is.
      match /ledger/{document=**} {
        allow read: if isHost(eventId) || isAdmin();
        allow write: if false;
      }

      // Deliberately unlisted, therefore denied: `hashes/` (the dedupe register — readable, it would
      // let anyone test whether a given photo was uploaded to an event).
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- root collections: all denied
    //
    // `claimLinks/{hash}` holds album-recovery link hashes — readable, it would turn a database dump
    // into working links; redemption goes through `POST /v1/claim`. `platform/*` holds the live-event
    // counter, the public-creation kill switch (spec 11 §1) and the director-tick heartbeat.
    // `publisherLease/*` is the kiosk's leader election (spec 04 §4) and `ticks/*` the director's tick
    // lease (spec 05 §1). None of them has a client use case, so none of them gets a rule — a lease a
    // client could write is not a lease.
  }
}
